{"name": "Pindari Glacier Trek", "slug": "pindari-glacier-trek", "type": "activity", "curatedDistrict": "Bageshwar", "region": "Kumaon", "location": "Loharkhet / Khati, Bageshwar", "category": "Trekking", "highlightCandidates": ["Kafni", "Zero Point", "Khati"], "articleTitle": "Pindari Glacier", "site": "wikipedia", "wikidataQid": "Q3776662", "coords": {"lat": 30.3, "lon": 80.02}, "onPageImages": ["File:Blue pog.svg", "File:Commons-logo.svg", "File:India Uttarakhand relief map.svg", "File:India Uttaranchal locator map.svg", "File:India relief location map.jpg", "File:Kafni glacier.jpg", "File:OOjs UI icon edit-ltr-progressive.svg", "File:PINDARI GLACIERMAPENGLISH made by Sumita Roy.png", "File:Pindari glacier, Uttarakhand, India.jpg"], "pageimage": "Pindari_glacier,_Uttarakhand,_India.jpg", "pageUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pindari_Glacier", "extract": "The Pindari Glacier is a glacier found in the upper reaches of the Kumaon Himalayas, to the southeast of Nanda Devi and Nanda Kot.\n\n\nGeography\nThe glacier is about 9 kilometers long   and gives rise to the Pindar River which meets the Alakananda at Karnaprayag in the Garhwal district.\nThe trail to reach the glacier crosses the villages of Saung, Loharkhet, crosses over the Dhakuri Pass, continues onto Khati village (the last inhabited village on the trail), Dwali, Phurkia and finally Zero Point, Pindar, the end of the trail. Though most of the trail is along the banks of the Pindari River, the river is mostly hidden until after Khati.\nThe Pindari Glacier trail provides for a 90 km (56 mi) round-trip trek that most people find comfortable to complete in 6 days. The Pindari Glacier is also famous for other adventure sports like Ice climbing and Mountain biking.\n\n\nRetreat\n\nSeveral surveys have mapped the retreat of Pindari over the years. The glacier was first surveyed by G.de P.Cotter in 1906. A 1958 survey by Amber P Tiwari  and Jangpangi in connection with International Geophysical Year, recorded a retreat of 1,040 m (3,410 ft) in the fifty two years since 1906. A 1966 survey recorded a further retreat of 200 m (660 ft) and discovered that a branch of the glacier, the Chhanguch branch, had separated and formed a separate ice shelf. As a result of the separation, the glacier lost several thousand cubic meters of ice. Recent studies have shown that the glacier has retreated an additional 1,569.01 meters between 1976 and 2014, possibly because of climate change.\nThis accelerating retreat, along with the retreat of other Himalayan glaciers, is likely to have an adverse impact on agriculture in the entire Ganges region since the Pindar river feeds the Alaknanda river, a headstream of the Ganges.\n\n\nSee also\nList of glaciers\nKafni Glacier\n\n\nReferences", "wikivoyageExtract": null, "wikidataDistrict": ["Q1499"], "coverImage": {"url": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Pindari_glacier%2C_Uttarakhand%2C_India.jpg/1920px-Pindari_glacier%2C_Uttarakhand%2C_India.jpg", "source": "Wikimedia Commons", "sourcePage": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pindari_glacier,_Uttarakhand,_India.jpg", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0)", "attribution": "Yann ( talk ) — Own work", "alt": "Pindari glacier, Uttarakhand, India."}, "coverImageLowerResolution": false, "gallery": [{"url": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Pindari_Glacier%2C_Bageshwar_in_Indian_Himalayas.jpg/1920px-Pindari_Glacier%2C_Bageshwar_in_Indian_Himalayas.jpg", "source": "Wikimedia Commons", "sourcePage": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pindari_Glacier,_Bageshwar_in_Indian_Himalayas.jpg", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0)", "attribution": "Harshit SR — Own work", "alt": "Pindari Glacier, Bageshwar, Uttarakhand, India"}, {"url": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Pindari_Glacier%2C_Bageshwar%2C_in_Indian_Himalayas%2C_Uttarakhand.jpg/1920px-Pindari_Gla